  "worked_solution": "By denoting $P(a,b)$ and $Q(a,b)$ as\n\n- $P(a,b):\\,a≡b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,2)$\n\n- $Q(a,b):\\,a≡b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,8)$\n\nour statement can be written as\n\n$$\n∀a,b\\in Z,\\,P⇒Q.\n$$\n\nWe want to disprove this statement. In other words, we must show that its negation is true:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}¬(∀a,b\\in Z,\\,P⇒Q) & ≡\\,∃a,b\\in Z,\\,¬(P⇒Q) \\\\  & ≡\\,∃a,b\\in Z,\\,¬(¬P∨Q) \\\\  & ≡\\,∃a,b\\in Z,\\,P∧¬Q \\\\  & ≡\\,∃a,b\\in Z,\\,(a≡b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,2))\\,∧\\,(a≢b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,8))\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we begin as follows:\n\nTo disprove a universal statement, it's sufficient to find a counterexample. \nIn other words, we need to show that there exist integers $a$ and $b$ such that \n\n$$\na≡b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,2)\\,\\text{and}\\,a≢b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,8).\n$$\n\nFinding a counterexample is sometimes a nontrivial task, but we only need one. In this case, substituting $(a,b)=(0,2)$ suffices. \n\nLet's choose\n\n$$\na=0,\\,b=2.\n$$\n\nTo check that this is a required counterexample, we proceed as follows:\n\nHence, we have that \n\n$$\n0≡2\\,(\\text{mod}\\,2)\\,\\text{and}\\,0≢2\\,(\\text{mod}\\,8).\n$$\n\nFinally, we state our conclusion:\n\nSo, the pair $a=0$ and $b=2$ is our counterexample. Therefore, the given statement is false.",
